      bgImageUrl: "data:image/png;base64,",
      notes: "PURPOSE: Show where a bank's funds come from and how deposits turn into income.\n\nFACILITATION GUIDANCE:\n- Read the definition banner, then walk the hierarchy: liabilities split into customer deposits (in red, the most important group), borrowings, and bonds & debentures.\n- SPOT THE CASA: ask learners which customer deposits make up CASA, then click each deposit on the slide. Savings and Current Accounts turn blue (\"CASA \u2713\"); Fixed and Recurring Deposits turn red (\"Not CASA \u2717\"). Current Accounts + Savings Accounts together form CASA.\n- Use the strip at the bottom to explain the cycle: deposits are collected, deployed as loans and investments, and generate income and growth.\n- Ask: \"Of these sources, which one do you think costs the bank the least?\" Hold the answer for the next slide.\n\nSUGGESTED ANSWERS / DEBRIEF POINTS:\n- Customer deposits, especially CASA, are the cheapest; borrowings and bonds usually cost the bank more.\n\nCOMMON MISCONCEPTIONS:\n- Learners may think \"liabilities\" is negative. In banking, liabilities are the bank's sources of funds, and deposits are its most important liability.\n- FDs and RDs are customer deposits but are not part of CASA."
