      notes: "PURPOSE: Compare a high-CASA bank with a low-CASA bank to show why CASA drives profitability.\n\nFACILITATION GUIDANCE:\n- PREDICT, THEN REVEAL: the rows appear with \"?\" in each cell. For each row, ask learners to predict Higher or Lower for a high-CASA and a low-CASA bank, then click once to reveal that row (4 clicks). A fifth click reveals the key takeaway.\n- Answers: a high-CASA bank has a lower cost of funds, higher lending capacity, higher NIM and higher overall profitability.\n- Link back to the previous slide: cheaper funds give a wider interest spread.\n- Close on the key takeaway: every employee's CASA effort matters from Day 1.\n- Ask: \"Why does a lower cost of funds lead to higher lending capacity and profitability?\"\n\nSUGGESTED ANSWERS / DEBRIEF POINTS:\n- Cheaper funds let the bank lend competitively while keeping a healthy margin, so it can grow its loan book and its profits.\n\nCOMMON MISCONCEPTIONS:\n- \"Lower\" is not always bad: for cost of funds, lower is the favourable outcome (shown with a green tick)."
